bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=source/lang value=ts
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=source/specifiers value=../lattice/forms.ts|../lattice/lattice.ts
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=source/answers value="what is determined by what"
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=source/head value="\n * A Galois connection between two orders, and the closure it induces: a form and the checks it derives, a set of\n * names and what they reach, a set of values and the laws they satisfy. It answers what is determined by what: the\n * closure of a body of claims, and the extension of a form.\n *\n * Which operation answers this view is read off the laws its vectors fix: the view an operation has is the view of\n * the laws that name the vectors it ran on, or of the definition it implements. An operation no law reaches keeps the\n * view it was given by hand, as drift. Nothing here is new algebra: every name is re-exported once.\n "
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/customised value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/dynamic value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/lexed value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/lines value=11..11
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/longest value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:01772e8499fbb998d7b6af0dd85d10e1cc18f26017b519830684f287abd40420 by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/unread value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:039df3b08dac46c481702bd8e7106db1d21f62be13c7f845479f5ff6dda5d7d4 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases|chain|coverage|preserves
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:039df3b08dac46c481702bd8e7106db1d21f62be13c7f845479f5ff6dda5d7d4 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="ANY of these, exactly, as a list.\n\nCombining says all of them must hold and lands on one range. `Any of them`\ndoes not: two ranges with a gap between them are not one range. So the\nanswer is a LIST — merged where merging picks up nothing neither side\naccepted, left apart where it would.\n\nA single range back means the alternatives really were one. More than one\nmeans they are genuinely separate.\n\nThe order is by floor, then by ceiling, so two implementations return the\nsame list rather than the same set."
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:03b4105963aae836034fef69f7788125a10ee66e477bc88812ad84ce17f60772 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases|coverage|widen
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:03b4105963aae836034fef69f7788125a10ee66e477bc88812ad84ce17f60772 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="What conserves, and where.\n\n`complexity` counts how much a statement says, and that count is preserved\nwhen two statements are put together: what BOTH require plus what EITHER\naccepts equals the sum of the parts. It is inclusion-exclusion, and it is\nwhat makes the count a measure rather than an index.\n\nOn ideals the count conserves on every finite poset: the up-sets of any\nposet form a distributive lattice. `isDistributive` still reports whether\nthe LEVEL view is faithful (a ladder). The falsifier that used to live\nhere is L0 — a non-closed pair is refused — and lives in ideals.json.\n\nON A LADDER THIS LAW IS TRIVIAL. For a total order the highest and lowest\nof two levels are just those two levels again, so ANY weighting satisfies\nit and nothing is being tested. The cases below that carry weight are the\nones off a ladder. M3 now conserves because the valuation counts excluded\nlevels of a declared Ω, not irreducibles."
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:03d4b22112e6999fa758f895aef0835ae989bfe76762b9961ec93695d3fb6cce by=reader:76e1db421edb form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=source/calls value=Array|Math|bin|claims|coherence|meet|next|now|out|seen|weight|where|width
