bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5c48711368bb6f4f504352074504461d7cf5101c78d70d95ecdd33dda30548ce by=reader:76e1db421edb form=interval measure=count role=reads scope=source/unread value=0..0
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5cc2c93fc44df0d7faf6b0095ae93d42f3ad4d5b8fa3453e70b4e34bc58d29a9 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5cc2c93fc44df0d7faf6b0095ae93d42f3ad4d5b8fa3453e70b4e34bc58d29a9 by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="For a budget of origins there is a siting that minimises what the field cannot reach, and it is found by asking which two islands are largest, because what an origin is worth is the product of the two sides it bridges.\nThree hundred cells, three cities bridged into one mass and two deserts of lone cells, leave four thousand three hundred and eighty pairs with no walk between them.\nTwelve origins dropped blindly close between none and a fifth of that. The same twelve, chosen by reach, close four fifths."
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5cc9d902a314bd5df43a5e510cc1860e80fc1bc4f5ac0fb8d3ce7b634005cacc by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases|coverage
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5cc9d902a314bd5df43a5e510cc1860e80fc1bc4f5ac0fb8d3ce7b634005cacc by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="Drift is not an error: one question with several homes is a point with several origins. It becomes an error only\nwhere the homes stop agreeing, and then the point splits and names the pair — the place where a copy went wrong."
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5dd71c2ef342b2a33268c91762cf1af64f1fc17daabb030c1c4aea299ff574df by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5dd71c2ef342b2a33268c91762cf1af64f1fc17daabb030c1c4aea299ff574df by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="A claim carries a size and a direction: how much it narrows, and the turn of the period it landed on. A withdrawal is the same size pointing the other way.\nTwo hundred claims in phase make ten thousand — the square of their number — and the same two hundred strewn across the period make sixty-four, of the order of their number.\nA write and its withdrawal at one epoch leave nothing; at different epochs they do not cancel.\nThirty-two claims of no width, so each carries an amplitude of one: in phase they make a thousand and twenty-four, which is their number squared, and strewn across the period they make fifty, of the order of their number.\nThe fringe of four claims half a period apart runs from nothing at no delay to four at half the period and back."
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5e46793066cbbdfd90849baf2d60aa87384dac83fc30b1f6e4ad93e0569c89ce by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=id role=reads scope=said/fields value=about|cases
bound-lock/1 at=place:sha256:5e46793066cbbdfd90849baf2d60aa87384dac83fc30b1f6e4ad93e0569c89ce by=reader:95297fcf0823 form=alphabet measure=text role=reads scope=said/about value="Three hundred cells of sixty-four states each, one observer of twenty looks an epoch paying each look from its own ceiling, and a look forgotten ten epochs after it was taken, over two hundred epochs."
